! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1260's INDICATOR-WALK DRIVER -- the six
! half-words that set one indicator, set its mask bit, enable, and
! await the interruption.  PDF p.45 (printed p.42), octal
! 021376.40-021402.00, transcribed VERBATIM at the listing's own
! addresses and checked bit-exactly by verify_ax5b_16087_listing.py.
!
! THIS IS THE BLOCK docs/NEXT.md HAS POINTED AT SINCE 2026-09-21.  The
! write-up's routine index says 1260 checks "the ability to interrupt
! ... unique interruption for bits 0-47", and this is where it does
! it: the deck's own version of what programs/interrupt_pg0.strap
! does hand-written from Reference Manual p.48.
!
!     I6087  SIC,I60SX1          -TURN ON BIT 20-47.
!            B,I60S1
!            TI,1,BIT0(X1),$IND  -SET ONE OF 20-47
!     I6088  TI,1,BIT0(X1),$MASK -SET MASK BIT OF TEST IND BIT
!            BE,$+.32            -ENABLE AND AWAIT INTERRUPT
!            BD,I60B13(X6)
!
! Set indicator bit N in word 11.  Set mask bit N in word 12.  Enable.
! If the machine interrupts, control never reaches the BD; if it does
! not, the BD dispatches into the per-indicator error table I60B13,
! indexed by X6, whose entries read "FAILED TO INTERRUPT ON IND MK /
! IN 1 MILLISEC.", then IK, IJ, EK, TS, CPUS, EKJ, UNRJ, CBJ, EPGK,
! UK, EE, EOP, CS.  p.48's three conditions -- mask bit one, indicator
! bit one, mechanism enabled -- supplied one indicator at a time.
!
! IT NEEDED AN INSTRUCTION THIS ASSEMBLER DID NOT HAVE.  TI is
! TRANSMIT IMMEDIATE, and its format came from the POCKET CARD, not
! from this listing (AGENTS.md rule 4).  X22-6533's pcard-1 gives the
! source form -- "TRANSMIT--Strap Format: OP, J, A1(I1), A2(I2)" --
! and the eight mnemonics T/TI/TB/TBI and SWAP/SWAPI/SWAPB/SWAPBI.
! pcard-3's "TRANSMIT OPERATIONS" box gives the word:
!
!   |   ADDRESS   |1000| I |   ADDRESS   | J |FD|BI|10| I |
!   0            18  24   28  32          51  55    58 60 63
!
!   SWAP  ab110     Instr'n Modifiers = ab
!   T     ab010       a = 0, forward       a = 1, backward
!                     b = 0, direct count  b = 1, immediate count
!
! That layout, written into opcodes.TRANSMIT_CODES and
! strap_asm.enc_transmit, reproduces BOTH printed half-words of the
! rows below with nothing read off this page.  A1 is a 24-bit BIT
! address, A2 a 19-bit HALF-WORD address, J four bits -- and with the
! immediate modifier J IS the count, so the encoder refuses a J that
! does not fit rather than truncating it.
!
! THE TWO TI ROWS ARE FULL WORDS AT ODD HALF-WORDS, 021377.40 and
! 021400.40, each straddling a word boundary (Ref. Manual p.39).  That
! is what made this page look a half-word short for a whole session
! when its addresses were read at 200 dpi; see ax5b_16086_ds.strap.
!
! WHAT THE 600 DPI RE-READ CORRECTED.  The last row's machine code is
! "21402.44 06", not "...00": at 200 dpi the low digit was lost under
! an ink smudge.  With 06 the row decomposes cleanly -- bit 18 set so
! the target is 021402.40, code 001 = BD, literal 000000, index 0110 =
! $X6 -- and 021402.40 is exactly where the page prints the label
! I60B13.  Every field of this block now agrees with every other.
!
! THREE EXTERNAL SYMBOLS THIS BLOCK LOCATES, each from its own row's
! printed address field:
!   I60SX1 = 023041.00   (SIC,I60SX1 -> "23041.00 80")
!   I60S1  = 022741.00   (B,I60S1    -> "22741.10 00")
!   I60B13 = 021402.40   (BD,...     -> "21402.44 06")
! docs/NEXT.md carried "I60S1/I60SX1 ... still not located" from the
! day the file's claim about them was disproved; they are located now,
! and I60SX1 is the same word the I60K constant table is based at (see
! docs/archival_inventory.md).
!
! AND BIT0 A THIRD TIME.  The first TI's A1 prints as 24522.00, so
! BIT0 is at 024522.00 -- which p.114's constants table prints
! directly and which I6071's BIT18 (024544.00, 18 words along)
! implies.  Three independent routes to one address.
!
! A HAZARD THIS TRANSCRIPTION CREATES, stated because the assembler
! can now emit Transmit at all: StretchExecute.bsv's Transmit arm
! moves exactly ONE word and ignores both the immediate-count bit and
! J, so a count other than 1 would assemble correctly and execute
! wrongly rather than failing loud.  Every count here is 1, so
! nothing in this file is affected; the note belongs with the encoder
! and is repeated in opcodes.TRANSMIT_CODES.
!
! WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  An ENCODING oracle, like the other four
! blocks of this routine.  It cannot run: I60S1 is a subroutine that
! is not transcribed, I60B13's table is not transcribed, and X1/X6
! are set up by code outside this range.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: one, where this header used to name two.  (1)
! RETIRED.  It used to say the deck's numeric labels (16087, 16088,
! 160SX1, 160S1, 160B13) are prefixed with "L" where they begin with a
! digit, because this assembler reads a leading digit as an address.
! They do not begin with a digit: the glyph was measured at 600 dpi
! and it is the LETTER I, so the deck's own I6087, I6088, I60SX1,
! I60S1 and I60B13 are legal STRAP-1 symbols unaided and are written
! here as the page prints them (docs/listing_typography.md).
! (2) The listing's "%...@" index parentheses are written "(...)",
! the same restoration ibox1_sic_cb_cbz.strap describes.

! External symbols, given by SYN at the addresses this block's own
! rows print for them.  Rows naming one check the opcode and modifier
! bits; their address came from the same print.
I60SX1 SYN, (8)23041.0            ! the SIC return link
I60S1  SYN, (8)22741.0            ! the save/restore subroutine
BIT0    SYN, (8)24522.0            ! the bit-mask constant table
I60B13 SYN, (8)21402.40           ! the per-indicator error table

        SLC, (8)21376.40
I6087  SIC, I60SX1               ! -TURN ON BIT 20-47.
        B,   I60S1
        TI,  1, BIT0($X1), $IND    ! -SET ONE OF 20-47
I6088  TI,  1, BIT0($X1), $MASK   ! -SET MASK BIT OF TEST IND BIT
        BE,  $+.32                 ! -ENABLE AND AWAIT INTERRUPT
        BD,  I60B13($X6)          !  reached only if it did NOT
        END, I6087
